#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673 :topic/id #uuid "b3c9ca33-97a8-546e-af44-a9a7a7a71eb5" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054384 :question/id #uuid "8955a3ce-a161-5dc5-8ec0-c357a0403b46" :question/math-academy-id "e-12862" :question/problem "Solve the equation $3p-7 =20.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $7$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\t3p-7 &=20\\\\\n\t\t3p-7+7 &=20+7 \\\\\n                3p + 0 &= 27 \\\\\n\t\t3p &=27\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t3p &=27 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{3p}{3} &=\\dfrac{27}{3} \\\\[5pt]  \n\t\t\\dfrac{\\cancel{3}p}{\\cancel{3}} &= 9 \\\\[5pt]  \n\t\tp &=9\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b170bf27-76bc-5558-9133-e81a4760072f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations that Involve Multiplication"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054385 :question/id #uuid "074b5213-4f4e-5d1b-a5c4-8b5403901b4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12863" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $2=5x-8.$" :question/worked-solution "To save time, we will keep the variable on the right-hand side as we solve. First, we apply the addition principle, adding $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =5x-8 \\\\ 2+8 & =5x-8+8 \\\\ 10 & =5x+0 \\\\ 10 & =5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =5x \\\\ \\frac{10}{5} & =\\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2=x,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52c2b31a-9b7a-52cb-9fa7-111de8bc53ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations while Keeping the Variable on the Right-Hand Side"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054386 :question/id #uuid "1f5b9030-a5de-5ac2-95c4-2fbaf638cdab" :question/math-academy-id "e-12864" :question/problem "Solve the equation $7-\\dfrac{p}{3} =9.$" :question/worked-solution "After converting the equation to a more familiar form, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n               7-\\dfrac{p}{3} &=9 \\\\\n    \t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+7 &=9\\\\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+7-7 &= 9-7 \\\\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3}+0 &= 2 \\\\\n\t\t-\\dfrac{p}{3} &=2\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle. We multiply by $-1$ to get rid of the negative sign, and again by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n               -\\dfrac{p}{3} &=2 \\\\\n              (-1) \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{p}{3} \\right) &=(-1) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n\t\t\\dfrac{p}{3} &=-2 \\\\\n\t\t\\cancel{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{p}{\\cancel{3}} &=3 \\cdot (-2) \\\\\n\t\tp &=-6\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac53e171-0a02-5b74-8a3a-465dbecda9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations that Involve Division"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054387 :question/id #uuid "6ed7ae7f-2591-5747-9fa0-933260aaaebf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12866" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\dfrac{z}{3}-\\dfrac 1 2 =\\dfrac 7 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3}-\\dfrac 1 2 &=\\dfrac 7 2\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} - \\dfrac 1 2+\\dfrac 1 2 &= \\dfrac 7 2+\\dfrac 1 2 \\\\[5pt]\n-\\dfrac{z}{3} + 0 &= \\dfrac 7 2+\\dfrac 1 2 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=\\dfrac 8 2 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=4\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle. We multiply by $-1$ to get rid of the negative sign, and again by $3$ to get rid of the fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\dfrac{z}{3} &=4 \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{z}{3} \\right) &= (-1) \\cdot 4 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{z}{3} &=-4 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\cancel{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{z}{\\cancel{3}} &= 3 \\cdot (-4) \\\\[5pt]   \n\t\tz &=-12\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82077653-3463-5132-b0a9-8376fdb47319" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations that Involve Fractions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 66 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050532}] :topic/title "Solving Two-Step Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814 :topic/difficulty 0.4298039530009263 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428386 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1849->175. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049537} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "15c8115d-e746-5dd5-ab8a-ad335bac2d26" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119612 :question/id #uuid "13fa2a8c-de7c-59ee-a838-2997494edc56" :question/math-academy-id "e-4782" :question/problem "Let ![](images/3b/3b40aa273b0ea1138535c7bfc77c2c80ee16a14570a8cbf0e9306ea66ad6c5d0.svg) Find $\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{a}$ by $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}a & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ce19559-0f08-5ef7-9ce4-58e8e14094ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Multiple of a Vector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119802 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119613 :question/id #uuid "1e82713d-4063-5bcd-bc2e-1d35c831ab34" :question/math-academy-id "e-4783" :question/problem "Let ![](images/f3/f3118303ab755b84d1a8065e3fef0ff13c2ff44e3601c3aae0207022b64c09fa.svg) and ![](images/dd/dd226e3cb1952497d8f546e4ee73d4860f23ccda89662bc2a4012f583aa6dc24.svg) Find $\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply each component of $\\mathbf{a}$ by $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and each component of $\\mathbf{b}$ by $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Then, we add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}a+\\frac{1}{2}b & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2 \\\\ 2-3 \\\\ 4+1 \\\\ -3-4 \\\\ 1+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab0d2c2f-e7a4-51bb-a20a-f28c2f7ded8b" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Multiples of Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119614 :question/id #uuid "ba38ff28-b4f3-59b8-b18a-a278db26294d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4784" :question/problem "If ![](images/86/8639fb58fd989253bb0095f58c39189ce653683c908d22a787c7454447ae706c.svg) and ![](images/24/24145ff5ab9076ec70b5e8f57304bd93e507154b5ad7cb61b5a580445fdad3dd.svg) then what is $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-4)+(-5)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2+12-12-30 \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec386a91-fc52-56d8-8531-7e78a8464380" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dot Product of Two Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119615 :question/id #uuid "3ec5f9d5-327b-59e5-8df7-fb37d4054021" :question/math-academy-id "e-4785" :question/problem "Let be ![](images/87/870f39e6853ca1dc2013d9b82beb35e1533a3d4b5f2318cf607f1f19efdeb0d7.svg) and ![](images/9f/9f51885fedc5cf606a4e2c48663c5e85df144fe004adf7795677a3a4dc37218a.svg) If $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the equation $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 10\\cdot x+x\\cdot x+0\\cdot 8+5\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 10x+{x}^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=-5.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94f8e0e5-8d8a-516a-8f46-d76e94305df4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Quantity in an Expression Involving the Dot Product"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1849 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050972}] :topic/title "Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"}]}
